    explanation: "Revelation is apocalyptic (it uses visions and symbolism), prophetic and epistolary (a letter to real churches). Symbols such as the lamb, the dragon, the beast and numbers like seven and twelve draw on the Old Testament (Daniel, Ezekiel, Isaiah). Chapters 2-3 speak to seven churches with praise, rebuke and promises. The central picture is the risen Christ on the throne and the Lamb who was slain (chapters 4-5).\n\nChristians have read the book in four main ways: preterist (largely fulfilled in the first-century setting), historicist (events across Church history), idealist (timeless conflict between good and evil) and futurist (mainly future events at the end). They differ on details such as the millennium (20:1-6) but agree on the heart: Christ will return, evil will be judged, the dead will be raised and God will make all things new. Jesus said no one knows the day or hour (Matthew 24:36), so date-setting is unwise.",
